00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,120
In Burp Suite Enterprise Edition, you can use our GraphQL API to integrate with third-party

2
00:00:09,120 --> 00:00:16,000
applications. For web applications that send requests to the API using client-side JavaScript,

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:21,320
you need to whitelist the origin of these requests for cross-origin resource sharing.

4
00:00:22,680 --> 00:00:29,160
You will also need to whitelist your Jenkins or TeamCity URLs if you use our native plugins to

5
00:00:29,160 --> 00:00:36,760
integrate your CI-CD systems and wish to use the Burp site-driven scan option. Once you have logged

6
00:00:36,760 --> 00:00:43,080
into Burp Suite Enterprise as an administrator, go to the settings menu and choose network.

7
00:00:45,240 --> 00:00:51,400
In the allowed origins for GraphQL API section, enter the origin on which the other application

8
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:56,920
is running. Make sure that you include the URL scheme, domain name, and port.

9
00:00:56,920 --> 00:01:06,280
Add each entry on a new line. When you're happy with the details you have added, click Save and then

10
00:01:06,280 --> 00:01:12,600
you're ready to start testing your external application. If you still run into cause-related

11
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:18,600
issues, examine the origin header of the associated request and compare this to the URLs that you have

12
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:25,800
in the whitelist. The origin of incoming requests refers only to the URL scheme, domain name, and port.

13
00:01:26,520 --> 00:01:32,120
You cannot restrict this to specific subdirectories. For more granular control,

14
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:42,040
you need to deploy your application to a dedicated subdomain.

15
00:01:42,040 --> 00:01:49,960
you need to deploy your application to a new URL scheme. You need to deploy your application.

